//? Title: Ordinary and universal kriging
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Predict a spatial field with uncertainty, compare a constant unknown mean with a covariate trend, and check whether kriging variances stay calibrated when the variogram is estimated and when predictions extrapolate

Kriging is best linear unbiased prediction for spatially correlated data. To predict \(Z(s_0)\) from observations \(z\), ordinary kriging assumes an unknown constant mean and chooses weights \(\lambda\) that minimise the prediction variance subject to \(\sum\lambda_i=1\). Universal kriging assumes the mean is linear in known covariates, \(E\,Z(s)=x(s)^\top\beta\), and constrains \(X^\top\lambda=x_0\). Both have the generalised-least-squares form

\[
\hat Z(s_0)=x_0^\top\hat\beta+c_0^\top C^{-1}(z-X\hat\beta),\qquad
\sigma_K^2=C(0)-c_0^\top C^{-1}c_0+u^\top(X^\top C^{-1}X)^{-1}u,
\]

with \(u=x_0-X^\top C^{-1}c_0\). Here \(C\) and \(c_0\) are covariances from the variogram model, and the last term accounts for estimating \(\beta\). Simple kriging uses a known mean and drops that term.

`simple_kriging`, `ordinary_kriging` and `universal_kriging` return predictions, kriging variances and the GLS trend coefficients. Each works with a global neighbourhood or with the nearest `max_neighbours` observations within `max_distance`. `kriging_cross_validation` gives leave-one-out predictions, residuals and z-scores. The tests reproduce `gstat::krige` on the meuse data for simple, ordinary and universal kriging (with \(\sqrt{\text{dist}}\) as the covariate), for global and `nmax` neighbourhoods, and for spherical and exponential models. They also reproduce `gstat::krige.cv` for ordinary and universal kriging, all to \(10^{-9}\).

The study uses a field with a linear trend: \(Z(s)=0.15\,s_x+W(s)+\varepsilon\), where \(W\) is a Gaussian process with exponential covariance (partial sill 1, scale 3) and \(\varepsilon\) has nugget variance 0.1. There are 150 observations uniformly in \(x\in[0,15]\), \(y\in[0,20]\). Predictions are made at 40 random interior points in the same region and at 40 extrapolation points with \(x\in[15,20]\). Over 200 fields, the variogram is either the true one (oracle) or fitted to the data with `fit_variogram` (exponential, default weights). For universal kriging the fit uses the residuals of an ordinary least-squares trend in \(s_x\) and \(s_y\).

```{cpp, id=krige, file="figures/kriging.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/geostatistics.hpp>

#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
using Matrix = dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>;
using dm::stats::VariogramModel;
using K = dm::stats::VariogramModelKind;

const VariogramModel truth{
    .kind = K::exponential, .nugget = 0.1, .partial_sill = 1, .range = 3};

// Joint draw of 0.15 x + W + nugget at all rows of xy (observations and targets).
std::vector<double> field(dm::random::SplitMix64& rng, const Matrix& xy) {
    const std::size_t n = xy.rows();
    std::vector<double> l(n * n, 0.0), out(n, 0.0), z(n);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j <= i; ++j) {
            double s = std::exp(-std::hypot(xy(i, 0) - xy(j, 0), xy(i, 1) - xy(j, 1)) / 3);
            for (std::size_t k = 0; k < j; ++k)
                s -= l[i * n + k] * l[j * n + k];
            l[i * n + j] = i == j ? std::sqrt(s) : s / l[j * n + j];
        }
    for (double& v : z)
        v = rng.normal();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        for (std::size_t k = 0; k <= i; ++k)
            out[i] += l[i * n + k] * z[k];
        out[i] += 0.15 * xy(i, 0) + std::sqrt(0.1) * rng.normal();
    }
    return out;
}

Matrix rows(const Matrix& a, std::size_t from, std::size_t count) {
    Matrix out(count, a.cols());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i)
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < a.cols(); ++j)
            out(i, j) = a(from + i, j);
    return out;
}
Matrix linear_trend(const Matrix& xy) {
    Matrix x(xy.rows(), 3);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < xy.rows(); ++i)
        x(i, 0) = 1, x(i, 1) = xy(i, 0), x(i, 2) = xy(i, 1);
    return x;
}
VariogramModel fitted(const Matrix& xy, const std::vector<double>& v) {
    std::vector<double> bounds;
    for (int k = 0; k <= 12; ++k)
        bounds.push_back(0.8 * k);
    const auto bins = dm::stats::empirical_variogram(xy, v, {.boundaries = bounds});
    return dm::stats::fit_variogram(bins, {.kind = K::exponential}).model;
}

struct Score {
    double se[2]{}, cover[2]{}, z2[2]{};
    void add(const dm::stats::KrigingResult& k, const std::vector<double>& actual) {
        for (std::size_t t = 0; t < actual.size(); ++t) {
            const int part = t < actual.size() / 2 ? 0 : 1;
            const double e = actual[t] - k.prediction[t], sd = std::sqrt(k.variance[t]);
            se[part] += e * e;
            cover[part] += std::abs(e) <= 1.959964 * sd;
            z2[part] += e * e / (sd * sd);
        }
    }
};

int main() {
    dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(8);
    const std::size_t n = 150, m = 80;
    const char* names[5]{"Ordinary, fitted variogram", "Ordinary, true variogram",
                         "Universal, fitted residual variogram",
                         "Universal, true variogram",
                         "Ordinary, fitted, 16 nearest"};
    Score score[5];
    const int reps = 200;
    for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
        Matrix all(n + m, 2);
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n + m; ++i) {
            const bool extrapolate = i >= n + m / 2;
            all(i, 0) = extrapolate ? 15 + 5 * rng.uniform01() : 15 * rng.uniform01();
            all(i, 1) = 20 * rng.uniform01();
        }
        const auto z = field(rng, all);
        const Matrix xy = rows(all, 0, n), targets = rows(all, n, m);
        const std::vector<double> obs(z.begin(), z.begin() + n);
        const std::vector<double> actual(z.begin() + n, z.end());
        const Matrix x = linear_trend(xy), x0 = linear_trend(targets);
        // GLS with an identity covariance (pure nugget) is OLS: residuals for the
        // universal-kriging variogram.
        const VariogramModel white{.kind = K::exponential, .nugget = 1, .partial_sill = 0};
        const auto beta = dm::stats::universal_kriging(xy, obs, x, targets, x0, white)
                              .trend_coefficients;
        std::vector<double> resid(n);
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
            resid[i] = obs[i] - beta[0] - beta[1] * xy(i, 0) - beta[2] * xy(i, 1);
        const VariogramModel raw_fit = fitted(xy, obs), resid_fit = fitted(xy, resid);
        score[0].add(dm::stats::ordinary_kriging(xy, obs, targets, raw_fit), actual);
        score[1].add(dm::stats::ordinary_kriging(xy, obs, targets, truth), actual);
        score[2].add(dm::stats::universal_kriging(xy, obs, x, targets, x0, resid_fit),
                     actual);
        score[3].add(dm::stats::universal_kriging(xy, obs, x, targets, x0, truth), actual);
        score[4].add(dm::stats::ordinary_kriging(xy, obs, targets, raw_fit,
                                                 {.max_neighbours = 16}),
                     actual);
    }
    const double per = reps * (m / 2.0);
    std::println("| Method | RMSE, interior | 95% coverage, interior | "
                 "RMSE, extrapolation | "
                 "95% coverage, extrapolation | Mean z^2, extrapolation |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (int k = 0; k < 5; ++k)
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.2f} |", names[k],
                     std::sqrt(score[k].se[0] / per), score[k].cover[0] / per,
                     std::sqrt(score[k].se[1] / per), score[k].cover[1] / per,
                     score[k].z2[1] / per);

    // One field along the transect y = 10, observations within 1 of it shown as points.
    Matrix all(n + 201, 2);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
        all(i, 0) = 15 * rng.uniform01(), all(i, 1) = 20 * rng.uniform01();
    std::vector<double> tx;
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k <= 200; ++k) {
        tx.push_back(0.1 * k);
        all(n + k, 0) = 0.1 * k, all(n + k, 1) = 10;
    }
    const auto z = field(rng, all);
    const Matrix xy = rows(all, 0, n), line = rows(all, n, 201);
    const std::vector<double> obs(z.begin(), z.begin() + n), actual(z.begin() + n, z.end());
    const auto ok = dm::stats::ordinary_kriging(xy, obs, line, truth);
    const auto uk = dm::stats::universal_kriging(xy, obs, linear_trend(xy), line,
                                                 linear_trend(line), truth);
    std::vector<double> ok_lo, ok_hi, uk_lo, uk_hi, px, py;
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k <= 200; ++k) {
        ok_lo.push_back(ok.prediction[k] - 1.96 * std::sqrt(ok.variance[k]));
        ok_hi.push_back(ok.prediction[k] + 1.96 * std::sqrt(ok.variance[k]));
        uk_lo.push_back(uk.prediction[k] - 1.96 * std::sqrt(uk.variance[k]));
        uk_hi.push_back(uk.prediction[k] + 1.96 * std::sqrt(uk.variance[k]));
    }
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
        if (std::abs(xy(i, 1) - 10) < 1)
            px.push_back(xy(i, 0)), py.push_back(obs[i]);
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.ribbon(tx, ok_lo, ok_hi, {176, 72, 43, 0.18})
        .ribbon(tx, uk_lo, uk_hi, {26, 90, 147, 0.22})
        .line(tx, actual, "True field", {0, 0, 0}, 1.2)
        .line(tx, ok.prediction, "Ordinary kriging", {176, 72, 43}, 2)
        .line(tx, uk.prediction, "Universal kriging", {26, 90, 147}, 2)
        .points(px, py, "Observations within 1 of the transect", {80, 80, 80}, 5)
        .abline_v(15, {120, 120, 120})
        .y_limits(-3.5, 8.5)
        .title("Kriging along y = 10 with 95% intervals")
        .x_label("x (observations only for x < 15; right of the line is extrapolation)")
        .y_label("Z")
        .size(620, 420);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.save_svg("build/kriging.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Method | RMSE, interior | 95% coverage, interior | RMSE, extrapolation | 95% coverage, extrapolation | Mean z^2, extrapolation |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Ordinary, fitted variogram | 0.638 | 0.942 | 1.322 | 0.869 | 1.69 |
| Ordinary, true variogram | 0.635 | 0.952 | 1.441 | 0.813 | 2.13 |
| Universal, fitted residual variogram | 0.637 | 0.943 | 1.087 | 0.917 | 1.28 |
| Universal, true variogram | 0.632 | 0.954 | 1.077 | 0.941 | 1.07 |
| Ordinary, fitted, 16 nearest | 0.636 | 0.943 | 1.186 | 0.917 | 1.29 |
\image(figures/kriging.svg)
// result_end

Inside the data region all five predictors are practically equivalent: RMSE 0.63–0.64 and 95% intervals covering 94–95% of true values. The plug-in variogram costs very little. Within the range of the data, the local covariance structure dominates and the mean model hardly matters, because the trend is absorbed by nearby observations.

Extrapolation separates them. Ordinary kriging assumes a constant mean, so beyond the data its predictions revert to the GLS mean. In the figure the red curve flattens right of \(x=15\) while the true field keeps rising. Its intervals reach only 81% coverage with the true variogram, and the mean squared z-score of 2.1 shows they are too narrow. The fitted variogram gives somewhat better coverage (87%) for the wrong reason: the unremoved trend inflates the empirical variogram, which widens the intervals. Universal kriging extrapolates the linear trend and accounts for its estimation uncertainty through the \(u^\top(X^\top C^{-1}X)^{-1}u\) term. That term widens the blue band as the target moves away from the data, and it brings coverage to 94% with the true residual variogram and 92% with the fitted one. A 16-nearest-neighbour ordinary kriging partly mimics a trend by re-estimating the mean locally, and its coverage of 92% matches universal kriging with a fitted variogram. It cannot, however, continue the trend beyond the data.

>> Practical guidance

- Use ordinary kriging for interpolation within a dense network. Use universal kriging, or kriging of residuals, when a covariate or coordinate trend is real and predictions extend beyond the data or into sparse areas.
- Estimate the variogram from detrended residuals when using universal kriging. A variogram of the raw data includes the trend, and it overstates both the sill and the range.
- Check calibration by leave-one-out cross-validation. The mean error should be near 0, and the mean squared z-score near 1. Values well above 1 mean the intervals are too narrow.
- Treat kriging variances as conditional on the variogram. They do not include variogram estimation uncertainty, so they are optimistic for small samples. Simulation or Bayesian kriging accounts for it.
- Prefer local neighbourhoods for large data sets or non-stationary means. Keep enough neighbours (typically 16 to 50) for a stable trend estimate in each neighbourhood.

>> References

- G. Matheron (1963). Principles of geostatistics. *Economic Geology* 58(8), 1246-1266. doi:10.2113/gsecongeo.58.8.1246
- N. Cressie (1993). *Statistics for Spatial Data*, revised edition. Wiley. doi:10.1002/9781119115151
- M. L. Stein (1999). *Interpolation of Spatial Data: Some Theory for Kriging*. Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4612-1494-6
- E. J. Pebesma (2004). Multivariable geostatistics in S: the gstat package. *Computers & Geosciences* 30(7), 683-691. doi:10.1016/j.cageo.2004.03.012
